{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "\"the social network\" is a thought-provoking film that delves into the complexities of the human mind, ambition, and the consequences of success. directed by david fincher, the movie is a biographical drama that tells the story of mark zuckerberg, the founder of facebook, and the tumultuous journey that led to his rise to fame.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, as a sophomore at harvard university. he's a brilliant programmer, but also a socially awkward and introverted individual who struggles to connect with others. after a painful breakup with his girlfriend, mark creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes an instant hit, but also generates controversy and gets mark into trouble with the university administration.\n\nsoon after, mark is approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss, played by armie hammer, who propose an idea for a social networking site exclusively for harvard students. mark agrees to work on the project, but eventually creates his own version, which he calls \"thefacebook.\" the site quickly gains popularity, and mark becomes an overnight sensation.\n\nas the film progresses, we see mark's relationships with his friends and associates become increasingly strained. eduardo saverin, played by andrew garfield, is mark's initial business partner and friend, but their friendship deteriorates as mark becomes more and more consumed by his creation. the winklevoss twins also become embroiled in a lawsuit against mark, claiming that he stole their idea.\n\none of the standout aspects of \"the social network\" is its cinematography. fincher's direction is masterful, and the film's visuals are sleek and modern. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, adding to the overall tension and unease that pervades the movie.\n\nthe performances in the film are also noteworthy. eisenberg brings a nuanced and complex portrayal to mark zuckerberg, capturing both his intelligence and his awkwardness. garfield is equally impressive as eduardo saverin, conveying the character's vulnerability and naivety. hammer is convincing as the winklevoss twins, capturing their entitled and arrogant personas.\n\nthroughout the film, fincher explores themes of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success. mark zuckerberg is portrayed as a complex and multifaceted individual, driven by both a desire for connection and a need for validation. the film raises important questions about the nature of social media and its impact on our lives, as well as the personal costs of achieving success.\n\noverall, \"the social network\" is a gripping and thought-provoking film that offers a fascinating glimpse into the life of mark zuckerberg and the creation of facebook. with its impressive cinematography, outstanding performances, and nuanced exploration of complex themes, it's a movie that will continue to resonate with audiences for years to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.9773347973823547}, {"token": "the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -6.437280717364047e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " thought", "logprob": -0.6583003401756287}, {"token": "-prov", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oking", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.010901418514549732}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00408066064119339}, {"token": " delves", "logprob": -0.20673628151416779}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " complexities", "logprob": -1.3707308769226074}, {"token": " of", "logprob"